url":"https://api.github.com/users/Lucaskabela/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Lucaskabela/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Lucaskabela/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Lucaskabela/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Lucaskabela/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Lucaskabela/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Lucaskabela/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Lucaskabela/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-04-16T23:15:30Z","updated_at":"2026-04-16T23:15:30Z","body":"Claude says:\n\n```\nWhat the report describes: the DTensor(ACT) object is passed to compile directly, without to_local(). The ACT stays nested inside _local_tensor. The PR's flat scan at frontend_utils.py:72-77 never sees it, and the codegen at subclass_codegen.py:269-271 only emits trigger_wait() for      \n  top-level indices. The ACT's __torch_dispatch__ would normally save you — but Inductor triton kernels bypass dispatch, which is the exact runtime hazard #179849's commit message calls out (\"silent data corruption\") — just one layer deeper than the PR handles.\n                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  \n  So #179849 is the PR that introduced this partial fix. The nested DTensor(ACT) case is a gap in the same mechanism. \n```","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pytorch/pytorch/issues/comments/4264051314/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimize